{
  "source": "C. G. Jung, 'The Association Method', Clark University lectures (1909), published American Journal of Psychology 21 (1910). English rendering of the Zurich list used in Diagnostische Assoziationsstudien (1904-1906).",
  "notes": [
    "Order is part of the method. Jung alternated nouns, adjectives and verbs, and interleaved neutral words with emotionally loaded ones. Do not shuffle by default.",
    "'to cook' appears twice (positions 11 and 20) in the published 1910 list. Kept for fidelity; expose a config option to substitute the second occurrence if desired.",
    "Jung read each word aloud. The app may optionally speak the word via TTS; the reaction clock should start when the stimulus is delivered, not when the screen renders.",
    "meta[] carries part of speech, a theme, and 'loaded' (Jung's emotionally critical stimuli, interleaved among neutral ones). Theme tags are our editorial layer for clustering disturbances, not Jung's own labels."
  ],
  "unit": "Jung recorded reaction time in fifths of a second using a stopwatch. Store milliseconds; display fifths.",
